{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_007","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"Title: Information and Memory in Dynamic Resource Allocation","location_hint":"paragraph 20","char_start":894,"char_end":954,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_008","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"Authors: Kuang Xu , Yuan Zhong","location_hint":"paragraph 21","char_start":958,"char_end":988,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_009","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"View a PDF of the paper titled Information and Memory in Dynamic Resource Allocation, by Kuang Xu and Yuan Zhong","location_hint":"paragraph 22","char_start":992,"char_end":1104,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_010","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"Abstract: We propose a general framework, dubbed Stochastic Processing under Imperfect Information (SPII), to study the impact of information constraints and memories on dynamic resource allocation. The framework involves a Stochastic Processing Network (SPN) scheduling problem in which the scheduler may access the system state only through a noisy channel, and resource allocation decisions must be carried out through the interaction between an encoding policy (who observes the state) and allocation policy (who chooses the allocation). Applications in the management of large-scale data centers and human-in-the-loop service systems are among our chief motivations.","location_hint":"paragraph 24","char_start":1118,"char_end":1789,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_011","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"We quantify the degree to which information constraints reduce the size of the capacity region in general SPNs, and how such reduction depends on the amount of memories available to the encoding and allocation policies.","location_hint":"paragraph 25","char_start":1791,"char_end":2010,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_012","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"Using a novel metric, capacity factor, our main theorem characterizes the reduction in capacity region (under &#34;optimal&#34; policies) for all non-degenerate channels, and across almost all combinations of memory sizes.","location_hint":"paragraph 25","char_start":2011,"char_end":2233,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_013","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"Notably, the theorem demonstrates, in substantial generality, that (1) the presence of a noisy channel always reduces capacity, (2) more memory for the allocation policy always improves capacity, and (3) more memory for the encoding policy has little to no effect on capacity.","location_hint":"paragraph 25","char_start":2234,"char_end":2510,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_5d98fdba0757_014","source_id":"src_5d98fdba0757","source_hash":"f5281b6fbd7ca304e6fea621f1fe24f68c6949f2cf62adda63c2ccb857c859b5","text":"Finally, all of our positive (achievability) results are established through constructive, implementable policies.","location_hint":"paragraph 25","char_start":2511,"char_end":2625,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-07T19:13:01.564Z"}
